From d7e7f366852534f009014c36f0464f2b2c4a28c3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dgabehar <dgabehar@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 01:23:12 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] ggml-rpc: give the RPC client a bounded receive timeout and
 keepalive

Root cause (QA round 8, R1): K2-Horizon-7B against two CPU rpc-server
workers under the --split-balance memory-split fallback would hang or
disconnect, needing an external `timeout` to kill. rpc_dispatcher (the
RPC *client*, used by a llama-server master to talk to each --rpc
worker) set no receive timeout and no TCP keepalive on its outbound
connections: socket_t::connect() leaves both unset, and every
dispatcher call blocks on std::future::wait() with no deadline. The
*server* already protects itself this way against a vanished/idle
client (set_recv_timeout during the handshake, set_keepalive after --
see rpc_serve_client), but nothing protected the client against a
worker that accepts a request and then goes silently unresponsive
without closing the socket (e.g. a worker straining under an
oversized/lopsided layer share from the memory-split fallback, or any
other cause of a peer that's alive but stuck). That wedges the calling
thread -- split-balance calibration or the real model load -- forever.

Reproduced locally (workstation, CPU-only ASan build, two
ggml-rpc-server workers + llama-server master, K2-Horizon-7B,
systemd-run --user --scope -p MemoryMax=12G -p MemorySwapMax=0):
freezing one worker mid weight-transfer (SIGSTOP, so its socket stays
open but it never responds -- the same "alive but not answering"
shape the memory-split fallback can produce under real load) hung the
master indefinitely with GGML_RPC_CLIENT_TIMEOUT_SEC=0 (old behavior).
The frozen master's log stops exactly where R1's did (no `predicted`
line), and its stack sits in rpc_dispatcher::send()'s future.wait().
With the fix, the same freeze produces the exact log signature R1
captured -- `ggml-rpc.cpp:...: Lost the connection to the RPC server
(it exited, restarted or is unreachable) or it sent a malformed
response` / `recv failed (bytes_recv=0, size_to_recv=8)` -- but bounded
to the configured timeout instead of hanging forever.

Extensive attempts to reproduce an *unbounded* hang purely from
calibrating/loading K2-Horizon-7B on this workstation (no frozen peer)
did not succeed: --split-balance auto calibration and the real model
load both complete in ~2-2.5 minutes under ASan + 2 CPU workers, and a
real /completion request works correctly. This points at a genuinely
unresponsive/wedged peer (memory pressure, thrashing, or the chaotic,
OOM-killing session QA's own round-8 report documents) as the trigger,
not a protocol deadlock independent of peer health -- which is exactly
what this fix bounds.

Fix: rpc_dispatcher::start() now sets SO_RCVTIMEO and TCP keepalive on
the client connection right after connect(), before the HELLO
handshake, so the whole connection lifecycle is bounded. This only
bounds an individual blocking recv() while a request is outstanding --
a client with nothing in flight never calls recv() and is unaffected,
mirroring the server's own "a connected client may stay idle for as
long as it likes" policy for the reverse direction. Default 180s
(comfortably longer than any single op measured here, including a
~70s full per-device calibration made of many smaller ops);
GGML_RPC_CLIENT_TIMEOUT_SEC=0 restores the old unbounded wait.

New regression test tests/test-rpc-client-timeout.{cpp,sh}: a minimal
hand-rolled RPC "server" completes the real HELLO handshake, then goes
silent forever on the next request without closing the socket. The
real client backend (ggml_backend_rpc_get_device_memory) must fail
within a bounded window (checked against GGML_RPC_CLIENT_TIMEOUT_SEC=2)
instead of hanging; ctest's own TIMEOUT property is the backstop if a
future regression reintroduces the unbounded wait.

Verified: ctest -L main 60/60 and ctest -R split-balance 1/1 both pass
(ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0:abort_on_error=1, per this fork's own ASan
testing convention -- the RPC backend's buffer-type/device registries
are intentionally never freed, so leak detection must stay off for
these tests same as the LAYERCAL-05 test plan already specifies).

Assisted-by: Claude Sonnet 5
